{{Strong|''Symphyotrichum pilosum''}} (formerly ''Aster pilosus'') is a [[perennial]], [[Herbaceous plant|herbaceous]], [[flowering plant]] in the [[Asteraceae]] [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Native plant|native]] to central and eastern North America. It is [[Common name|commonly]] called {{Strong|hairy white oldfield aster}}, {{Strong|frost aster}}, {{Strong|white heath aster}}, {{Strong|heath aster}}, {{Strong|hairy aster}}, {{Strong|common old field aster}}, {{Strong|old field aster}}, {{Strong|awl aster}}, {{Strong|nailrod}}, and {{Strong|steelweed}}. There are two varieties: ''Symphyotrichum'' {{Nowrap|''pilosum'' var.}} ''pilosum'', known by the common names previously listed, and ''Symphyotrichum'' {{Nowrap|''pilosum'' var.}} ''pringlei'', known as {{Strong|Pringle's aster}}. Both varieties are [[NatureServe conservation status|conservationally secure]] globally and in most provinces and states where they are native.

The varieties differ in [[Morphology (biology)|morphology]], [[Species distribution|distribution]], and [[habitat]]; {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pilosum'' is hairy, and {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pringlei'' is hairless, or nearly so. {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pilosum'' is the more widespread of the two and grows in various dry habitats, often with [[weed]]s. {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pringlei'' grows in higher-quality [[Calcareous|calcium-rich]] [[ecosystem]]s, often with many non-weedy companion [[flora]]. {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum''}} has been [[Introduced species|introduced]] to several European and Asian countries.

The species usually reaches heights between {{Convert|20|cm|in|0|abbr=off|sp=us}} and {{Convert|120|cm|ft|0|abbr=in|sp=us}}. It blooms late summer to late fall with [[Pseudanthium|composite flowers]] that are {{Convert|0.5|to|0.75|in|mm|abbr=out|sp=us|order=flip}} wide and have white [[ray floret]]s and yellow [[disk floret]]s. {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pringlei'' has been used in the [[cultivar]] industry, and it and its cultivar 'Ochtendgloren' have won the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].
