==Description==
''Symphyotrichum pilosum'' is a [[Perennial plant|perennial]], [[Herbaceous plant|herbaceous]], [[flowering plant]] that reaches heights between {{Convert|20|cm|in|0|abbr=on}} and {{Convert|120|cm|ft|0|abbr=on}}; some plants can be as short as {{Convert|5|cm|in|0|abbr=on}} and others up to or taller than {{Nowrap|{{Convert|150|cm|ft|0|abbr=on}}.{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}}}} It flowers late summer to late fall, in the north ending in October and farther south in December.{{Sfnp|Chmielewski|Semple|2001|p=858}} 

There are two varieties: ''Symphyotrichum pilosum'' {{Nowrap|var. ''pilosum''}} and {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pringlei''. They differ in [[Morphology (biology)|morphology]], [[Ploidy|chromosome count]], [[Species distribution|distribution]], and {{Nowrap|[[habitat]].{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}}{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006a}}{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006b}}}} {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pilosum'' is the [[Autonym (botany)|autonym]], with hairy stems and leaves. It is widespread and often weedy.{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006a}} {{Nowrap|''S.'' ''pilosum'' var.}} ''pringlei'' is hairless, less common, and often grows in high-quality habitats.{{Sfnp|Wilhelm|Rericha|2017|p=1105}}

===Roots and stems===
The roots of ''S. pilosum'' have bulky and branched [[Caudex|caudices]] that sometimes have long [[rhizome]]s{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}} which store nutrients for the next year's flowering.{{Sfnp|Chmielewski|Semple|2001|p=858}} The plant is [[cespitose]] with one to five straight stems.{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}} The two varieties have different stem surfaces: {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pilosum'' has hairy stems ([[pilose]] to densely [[hirsute]]),{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006a}} and {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pringlei'' has stems with no hair ([[glabrous]]){{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006b}} or at most, they are hairy in vertical lines.{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}}

===Leaves===
{{Nowrap|''S. pilosum''}} has thin,{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}} [[Alternate leaf|alternate]], and [[Simple leaf|simple leaves]].{{Sfnp|NC State Extension|n.d.}} Those of {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pilosum'' are hairy,{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006a}} and those of {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum''}} var. ''pringlei'' are hairless or nearly hairless.{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006b}} Leaves grow at the base, on the stem, and in the inflorescence branches. The ground leaves ([[Basal leaf|basal leaves]]) are either [[Sessility (botany)|sessile]] or have very short [[Petiole (botany)|leafstalks]] with [[Leaf sheath|sheathing]] [[Wing (botany)|wings]] fringed with hairs on the [[Leaf margin|margins]] (edges), meaning they are [[Ciliate (leaf)|ciliate]]. They are [[oblanceolate]] or [[obovate]] to [[spatulate]] with [[Obtuse leaf|obtuse]] to [[Rounded leaf|rounded]] tips, and their bases are [[Attenuate leaf|attenuate]].{{Efn|group=n|[[File:Leaf morphology Attenuate.png|20px|alt=refer to definition]] ''[[Attenuate leaf|Attenuate base]]'' is defined as "[h]aving leaf tissue taper down the [[Petiole (botany)|petiole]] to a narrow base, always having some leaf material on each side of the petiole". {{Small|(Copied definition from [[Glossary of leaf morphology]]; see that page's history for attribution.)}}}} Their margins are slightly [[Crenate|wavy]] or [[Serrate leaf|saw-toothed]], mostly near the tips. Basal leaves range in lengths from {{Convert|10|to|60|mm|in|abbr=on|sigfig=1}} and widths from {{Convert|5|to|15|mm|in|abbr=on|sigfig=1}}. They grow in a [[Rosette (botany)|rosette]] and develop prior to flowering, then wither or die during plant growth. At the time of flowering, another rosette of basal leaves forms.{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}}

Lower stem leaves usually wither by flowering, and they often have small leaves in clusters at the stem [[Node (botany)|nodes]]. These stem leaves are [[Entire leaf|entire]] (smooth on the margins) to saw-toothed and have soft cilia. Their tips are attenuate, and the leaves are elliptic-oblanceolate, elliptic-oblong, linear-lanceolate, or linear-oblanceolate. The leaf bases can be petiolate or subpetiolate to subsessile, and they clasp the stem with attenuate to [[Cuneate leaf|cuneate bases]]{{Efn|group=n|[[File:Leaf morphology cuneate.png|30px|alt=refer to definition]] ''[[Cuneate leaf|Cuneate base]]'' is defined as "[t]riangular, wedge-shaped" where the leaf attaches to the stem. {{Small|(Part of this definition was copied from [[Glossary of leaf morphology]]; see that page's history for attribution.)}}}} with wings. Lengths are from {{Convert|40|to|102|mm|in|abbr=on|sigfig=2}} and widths from {{Convert|5|to|25|mm|in|abbr=on|sigfig=1}}, getting progressively smaller as they approach the upper portion of the stem.{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}}

[[Distal]] leaves, higher on the stem and on the branches with the [[flower head]]s, are lance-oblong, linear-lanceolate, linear, linear-oblanceolate, or linear-subulate. Their margins are entire or saw-toothed, and they are sessile with cuneate bases. Lengths range from {{Convert|10|to|100|mm|in|abbr=on|sigfig=1}} and widths are {{Convert|1|to|8|mm|in|abbr=on|sigfig=1}}. The leaves get progressively smaller near the [[inflorescence]]s; closest to the flower heads, their sizes are abruptly reduced.{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}}

