===Flowers===
{{See also|Asteraceae#Flowers}}
[[File:Symphyotrichum pilosum pringlei 92057395 (rotated).jpg|thumb|upright=0.7|alt=refer to caption|{{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pringlei'' inflorescence showing a branch with many peduncles, each with its own flower head. The inflorescence is in the shape of a pyramid.]]

The [[flower head]]s of ''Symphyotrichum pilosum'' are {{Convert|0.5|to|0.75|in|mm|abbr=on|order=flip}} wide{{Sfnp|NC State Extension|n.d.}} and grow on branches that are {{Convert|100|mm|in|0|abbr=on}} or less at wide angles off the stem ([[:wikt:divaricate|divaricate]]). They also may be ascending and arching. They are open, generally not crowded, and have many small leaves. Each flower head is on a [[Peduncle (botany)|peduncle]] that is usually {{Convert|5|to|30|mm|in|abbr=on|sigfig=2}}, sometimes as long as {{Convert|50|mm|in|0|abbr=on}}. They get shorter the farther up the branch they grow which then causes the [[inflorescence]] to look like a pyramid. The peduncles of {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum''}} var. ''pilosum'' have dense and bristly hairs ([[hispid]]), and those of {{Nowrap|''S. p.'' var.}} ''pringlei'' are hairless ([[Glabrousness|glabrous]]). Both varieties have from {{Nowrap|7 to 25}} or more [[bract]]s that are glabrous and may have cilia.{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}}

====Involucres and phyllaries====
[[File:Symphyotrichum pilosum cropped.jpg|thumb|upright=0.6|alt=side view several flower heads with one blooming|Flower heads]]
Flower heads of all members of the Asteraceae family have [[Phyllary|phyllaries]] which are small, specialized leaves that look like scales. Together they form the [[involucre]] that protects the individual flowers in the head before they open.{{Efn|group=n|See [[Asteraceae#Flowers|Asteraceae § Flowers]] for more detail.}}{{Sfnp|Morhardt|Morhardt|2004|p=29}} The involucres of ''Symphyotrichum pilosum'' are [[campanulate]] to [[Cylindrical (botany)|cylindro]]-campanulate in shape (bell to cylinder-bell) and usually {{Convert|3.5|to|5.1|mm|in|abbr=on|sigfig=2}} in length, although they can be as short as {{Convert|2.5|mm|in|abbr=on|sigfig=1}} and as long as {{Convert|6.5|mm|in|abbr=on|sigfig=2}}.{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}}

The phyllaries of {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pilosum'' have small and sparse hairs, meaning they are [[:wikt:hirsutulous|hirsutulous]], and those of variety ''pringlei'' are glabrous. Both varieties have phyllaries in {{Nowrap|4 to 6}} rows that are unequally aligned, and rarely subequally aligned. The phyllaries are appressed or slightly spreading, and the outer ones are [[Oblong (botany)|oblong]]-[[lanceolate]] in shape, while the inner are [[Linear leaf|linear]]. They have green [[:wikt:chlorophyllous|chlorophyllous]] zones that are lanceolate to lance-[[Rhombic leaf|rhombic]] with [[Apex (leaf)|apices]] (tips) that have either short and sharp points ([[Acute leaf|acute]]) or taper to long points ([[acuminate]]). Their margins appear white or light green, but they are actually translucent and may appear nibbled or worn away (in botanical terms, they are [[scarious]] and [[erose]]). They are [[Revolute leaf|inrolled]] at the tips.{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}}

====Florets====
[[File:Symphyotrichum pilosum 164939625.png|thumb|upright=0.6|alt=white ray florets and yellow disk florets|Close-up of flower head showing open and closed disk florets with elongated stamens, some with pollen still attached]]
Each flower head is made up of [[ray floret]]s and [[disk floret]]s. The {{Nowrap|16 to 28}}{{Efn|group=n|Outside range {{Nowrap|10 to 38}}{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}}}} ray florets grow in one series and are usually white, rarely pinkish or bluish. They are usually between {{Convert|5.4|and|7.5|mm|in|abbr=on}} in length, but can be as short as {{Convert|4|mm|in|abbr=on}} and as long as {{Convert|11|mm|in|abbr=on}}. They are {{Convert|0.4|to|1.7|mm|in|abbr=on}} wide{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}} and they make up about 45% of the total number of florets on a flower head.{{Sfnp|Chmielewski|Semple|2001|p=859}}

The disks have {{Nowrap|17 to 39}}{{Efn|group=n|Outside range {{Nowrap|13 to 67}}{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}}}} florets that start out as yellow and after opening, turn reddish purple or brown after [[pollination]]. Each disk floret is {{Convert|3.4|to|4.1|mm|in|abbr=on}} in depth, sometimes up to {{Convert|5.5|mm|in|abbr=on}}, and is made up of {{Nowrap|5 petals}}, collectively a [[Petal|corolla]], which open into 5 lanceolate lobes comprising {{Convert|0.4|to|1|mm|in|abbr=on|sigfig=1}} of the depth of the floret.{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}}

===Fruit===
The fruits of ''Symphyotrichum pilosum'' are not true [[achene]]s but are [[cypselae]], resembling an achene and surrounded by a [[Calyx (botany)|calyx]] sheath. This is true for all members of the Asteraceae family.{{Sfnp|Barkley|Brouillet|Strother|2006}} After pollination, they become off-white or gray with an oblong-obovoid shape, {{Convert|1|to|1.5|mm|in|abbr=on|sigfig=1}} in length, and {{Convert|0.5|to|0.7|mm|in|abbr=on|sigfig=1}} in width. They have {{Nowrap|4 to 6}} nerves and a few stiff, slender bristles on their surfaces ([[strigillose]]). Their [[Pappus (botany)|pappi]] (tufts of hairs) are white and {{Convert|3.5|to|4|mm|in|abbr=on}} in length.{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006}} The seeds mature late in the season but cannot [[Germination|germinate]] at low temperatures. They are dispersed by the fall and winter winds and germinate when temperatures become warm in the spring.{{Sfnp|Chmielewski|Semple|2001|p=858}}
[[File:Symphyotrichum pilosum pilosum fruiting 2022-11.jpg|thumb|upright=0.9|alt=plant has gone to seed; each flower head is covered in feathery-looking seeds|Fruiting plant showing many cypselae with pappi]]

===Chromosomes===
{{Nowrap|''S. pilosum''}} has a [[Ploidy#Haploid and monoploid|monoploid number]] (also called ''base number'') of eight [[chromosomes]] {{Nowrap|('''''x''''' {{=}} 8).}}{{Sfnp|Semple|2014c}} There are plants of {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pilosum'' with four and six sets of the chromosomes, [[tetraploid]] with 32 total<!--{{Nowrap|({{Strong|2''n''}} {{=}} 32)}}--> and [[hexaploid]] with 48 total<!--{{Nowrap|({{Strong|2''n''}} {{=}} 48)}}-->, respectively. These [[Karyotype|cytotypes]] occur regularly, with occasional [[pentaploid]]s (five sets) that have 40 in total<!--{{Nowrap|({{Strong|2''n''}} {{=}} 40)}}-->.{{Sfnp|Semple|2014a}} {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pringlei'' is hexaploid with a total chromosome count of 48<!-- {{Nowrap|({{Strong|2''n''}} {{=}} 48)}}-->.{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006b}}

Differences in morphology have been found between tetraploids and hexaploids of {{Nowrap|''S. pilosum'' var.}} ''pilosum'' in southwestern Ontario, including involucre and floret sizes,{{Sfnp|Semple|2014a}} branch length, leaf sizes, and others; they are consistently smaller on tetraploids than on hexaploids. Additionally, the leaf hair on tetraploids is denser than on hexaploids.{{Sfnp|Chmielewski|Semple|2001|p=856}}

==Taxonomy==
===Etymology===
The [[Specific epithet (botany)|specific epithet]], second part of the scientific name, ''pilosum'' is from [[Latin]] {{Wikt-lang|la|pilosus|pilosus}} meaning "with long soft hairs".{{Sfnp|Wilhelm|Rericha|2017|p=1104}} The species has the [[common name]]s of hairy white oldfield aster,{{Sfnp|USDA|2014}} frost aster, white heath aster, heath aster, hairy aster, common old field aster, and steelweed.{{Sfnp|Chmielewski|Semple|2001|p=851}} ''Symphyotrichum'' {{Nowrap|''pilosum'' var.}} ''pringlei'' is commonly known as Pringle's aster{{Sfnp|Brouillet|Semple|Allen|Chambers|2006b}} and was named for American [[botanist]] [[Cyrus Guernsey Pringle]].{{Sfnp|Wilhelm|Rericha|2017|p=1105}}

===Type specimens===
